    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `locality` field is a read-only value derived purely from an owner's `city`, so it belongs alongside the other computed response fields (`displayName`, `initials`, `membershipTier`) \u2014 mapped at response time, requiring no entity/DB/pipeline changes and working uniformly for both freshly-created and loaded owners.\n\n1. **`util/CityRegion.java`** (new) \u2014 a single-responsibility class holding the pinned city\u2192region table (`Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`) and one method, `localityOf(city)`, returning the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"`. Case-insensitive matching, mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode` city handling.\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `locality` mapping that delegates to `CityRegion.localityOf(...)` via a thin one-line `default` method, matching the pattern of the existing derived fields and keeping the table logic out of the mapper.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it in the JSON response.\n\nThe lookup table lives in its own reusable unit rather than inlined into the mapper, so no class became a catch-all.",
